## Decision Logging (logmind) — REQUIRED for substantive commits

> **DO NOT run `git add` / `git commit` / `git push` for substantive code changes.**
> `logmind log` is the commit primitive. Raw git commands for non-trivial changes
> bypass the decision tree, leaving future agents (and humans) without context.
> The commit-msg hook and the Claude Code PreToolUse hook installed by
> `logmind init` / `logmind doctor --fix` **BLOCK** a substantive commit that
> skips `logmind log` — they don't just warn. If a raw commit is genuinely
> correct (mechanical, no decision to record), add `[skip-logmind]` to the
> commit subject, or set `LOGMIND_ALLOW_GIT_COMMIT=1` for one command.
> `git.enforce_commits: false` in `.logmind/config.yml` disables enforcement
> for the whole repo.

**This project uses [logmind](https://logmind.dev). `logmind log` is the commit primitive — it replaces `git add`, `git commit`, and `git push` for any change that carries a decision. Do not run those git commands directly when logging a decision.**

> **Tip:** the canonical procedure for *when / how / why* lives in the
> [`logmind` skill](https://github.com/thrillmade/agent-skills/tree/main/skills/logmind)
> on skills.sh. If your agent runtime supports skills, install it once
> and you can keep the project-local section here lean:
>
> ```bash
> npx skills add https://github.com/thrillmade/agent-skills --skill logmind
> ```
>
> The full procedure is also embedded below as a fallback when the
> skill isn't available.

### The one rule: `logmind log` does everything

```bash
logmind log "decision summary" -r "why" -a "alternative" -i "implication"
```

That single command:

1. Writes the decision file — `docs/decisions-branches/<branch>.md` for whatever branch you are on, the default branch included (`main.md`).
2. Keeps the derived docs (`docs/timeline.md`, `docs/timeline-archive.md`, `docs/file-structure.md`) pinned to their merge-base with the default branch — a branch never modifies them, which is what makes concurrent PRs conflict-free. They regenerate on the default branch, from the git hooks and the `check-derived-docs` workflow.
3. **`git add`** every change in the working tree (default `--stage all` since v0.2.7).
4. **`git commit`** with message `logmind: <decision>`.
5. **`git push`** to remote (configurable in `.logmind/config.yml`).

You don't need a follow-up `git add`, `git commit`, `git push`, or `logmind timeline --write`. If you find yourself running any of those after a `logmind log`, stop — something's wrong (most likely a pre-v0.2.7 logmind; check `logmind --version`).

### When you MUST log (before, not after)

Log a decision **BEFORE** you do any of these — your reasoning is the
log entry, not a justification you write afterwards:

- Choose between alternative approaches (frameworks, libraries, patterns, algorithms)
- Write more than ~20 lines of new code, or a new module
- Modify existing functionality in a non-obvious way (refactors, API changes, behaviour changes)
- Add or remove a dependency
- Make any security or performance trade-off
- Rename or move a significant module / file / function
- Choose a data shape (schema, config format, output structure)

**Do not skip logging because the change "feels small."** Small changes
that don't get logged are exactly how teams lose context. If you're
unsure, log it — a 30-second log is cheap; recovering missing context
months later is not.

### When NOT to use `logmind log`

- **The change has no decision worth recording** (typo fix, formatting, dependency-only patch bump) — plain `git commit` is fine for those.
- **You have unrelated WIP in the working tree** you don't want to commit alongside the decision — pass `--stage scoped` to stage only the decision files (rare for automated agents; they should be working on one task at a time).

### How to log

**CLI (preferred):**
```bash
logmind log "Use PostgreSQL for primary database" \
  -r "Need ACID compliance and complex joins" \
  -a "MongoDB" -a "SQLite" \
  -i "Connection pooling required" \
  -i "Schema migrations via alembic"
```

### Branch-aware routing (automatic)

Every entry lands in `docs/decisions-branches/<sanitized-branch>.md` for
the branch it was made on — the default branch included, where the file
is `main.md`. One rule, no exception: the default branch is a branch like
any other. You don't manage this — `logmind log` does.

`docs/decisions.md` is a legacy source. Where it still exists it is read,
never written, except on the three paths that have no branch to route by:
`decisions.branch_aware: false`, a non-git directory, and a detached or
unborn HEAD.

### Branch summary (headline)

On a feature branch, set a one-sentence, plain-English summary of what
the WHOLE branch does — `logmind headline "<one sentence>"`, or bundle it
into a decision with `logmind log "..." -H "<one sentence>"`. It's stored
at the top of the branch's decision file and copied verbatim into
`docs/timeline.md`, so it's the FIRST thing the next agent reads. Refine
it as the branch grows (the entry key stays stable). It applies on every
branch — the default branch is a branch like any other.

### Required reading at task start

Before starting any non-trivial task in this repo, **read these in order**:

1. **[docs/timeline.md](docs/timeline.md)** — auto-generated chronological overview, the 50 most recent decisions across all branches. Start here for the lay of the land.
2. **`docs/decisions-branches/<your-branch>.md`** — decisions made earlier on the branch you're on, in full detail.
3. **[docs/file-structure.md](docs/file-structure.md)** — current project tree (regenerated on each main-branch log and after every PR merge).

```bash
logmind show               # recent decisions on the current branch
logmind show --all         # include every other branch's decision file
logmind search "postgres"  # full-text across the decision log
```

Working as an agent? Set `LOGMIND_QUIET=1` for terse, chainable machine output
on the read/emit verbs (`doctor`, `file-structure`, `guard-commit`, `headline`,
`log`, `repomap`, `search`, `show`, `timeline`): each suppresses progress
chatter and prints a single `ok <key=value>` line. Other verbs currently
ignore this flag.

Reading prior decisions first prevents you from re-litigating something
the team already decided.

### Additional reference

- **`docs/timeline-archive.md`** — the history older than the 50 entries in `docs/timeline.md`, same format (written by the same regeneration)
- **docs/decisions-branches/** — per-branch decision logs; the record itself, append-only and uncapped
- **README.md** at the repo root — project overview, if present
